Great 👍 Let’s look at 3 very important locals patterns used by DevOps engineers in real Terraform production projects, using examples with Amazon Web Services (AWS) infrastructure.

These patterns are commonly used when creating VPC, EC2, ALB, RDS, Kubernetes clusters, etc.

1️⃣ Dynamic Resource Naming Pattern (Very Common in Production)
In real companies, every resource follows a standard naming convention.

Example naming rule:

project-environment-resource
Example:

payment-prod-ec2
payment-prod-vpc
payment-prod-alb
Step 1 — variables.tf
variable "project" {
  default = "payment"
}

variable "environment" {
  default = "prod"
}
Step 2 — locals.tf
locals {

  name_prefix = "${var.project}-${var.environment}"

}
Step 3 — Use in Resources
Example EC2 instance in AWS.

resource "aws_instance" "app" {

  ami           = "ami-0f58b397bc5c1f2e8"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "${local.name_prefix}-ec2"
  }

}
Result:

payment-prod-ec2
Same prefix can be reused everywhere.

Example:

payment-prod-vpc
payment-prod-rds
payment-prod-alb
This is standard practice in large DevOps environments.

2️⃣ Multi-Environment Pattern (dev / qa / prod)
In real projects we deploy same infrastructure for multiple environments.

Example:

dev
qa
prod
Instead of changing values manually, we use locals with environment logic.

variables.tf
variable "environment" {
  default = "dev"
}
locals.tf
locals {

  instance_type = {
    dev  = "t2.micro"
    qa   = "t2.small"
    prod = "t3.medium"
  }

}
Use in Resource
resource "aws_instance" "app" {

  ami = "ami-0f58b397bc5c1f2e8"

  instance_type = local.instance_type[var.environment]

}
What Happens
If environment = dev

instance_type = t2.micro
If environment = prod

instance_type = t3.medium
So same Terraform code works for all environments.

This pattern is heavily used in companies using Terraform + AWS.

3️⃣ Locals With Loops (Creating Multiple Resources)
In production we often create multiple servers automatically.

Example:

web-1
web-2
web-3
Instead of writing 3 EC2 resources manually, we generate them using locals.

locals.tf
locals {

  servers = {
    web1 = "t2.micro"
    web2 = "t2.micro"
    web3 = "t2.small"
  }

}
main.tf
resource "aws_instance" "web" {

  for_each = local.servers

  ami           = "ami-0f58b397bc5c1f2e8"
  instance_type = each.value

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }

}
Terraform Automatically Creates
web1
web2
web3
Instances will have:

web1 → t2.micro
web2 → t2.micro
web3 → t2.small
This pattern is very powerful for automation.

